WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:06.990
It is Thursday, which means we have the latest
jobs numbers and they're not good when we

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:12.970
add the jobs lost in the last week or last
week, I guess I would say that puts the total

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:17.310
number of jobs lost during the seven weeks
of what we would, I guess call the Corona

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:18.310
virus.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.770
Quarantine's at thirty three and a half million
jobs last week.

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:29.440
The latest numbers, about 3.2 million more
people lost their jobs and filed for unemployment.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:35.730
Now, if you are looking at these numbers and
you are determined to find some okay news

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:41.470
or some sign of it's not as bad as it could
be, what you could say and you would be right

00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:48.340
to say it is that each day I'm sorry, each
week fewer people are losing their jobs.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.579
We had more than 6 million people lose their
jobs in the first in

00:00:52.579 --> 00:00:53.600
the last week of March.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.559
The first week of April, only about half of
that number.

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:03.100
Each week over the last couple of weeks have
lost their jobs in the 3 million job range.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.609
But in total, these are unconscionable numbers.

00:01:05.609 --> 00:01:09.340
Now, to give you a little bit of context before
this all started.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:14.160
So seven weeks ago, eight weeks ago, the U.S.
workforce was believed to be about one hundred

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.670
and fifty eight million people.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.299
Hundred and fifty eight million people were
formerly in the workforce in the United States

00:01:20.299 --> 00:01:21.670
before this all started.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.810
So when you look at 33 and a half million
jobs.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:33.180
That is 21 percent of the labor force of the
United States that has lost their jobs, not

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.469
in a year, which would be a tragedy in seven
weeks.

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:38.759
In seven weeks.

00:01:38.759 --> 00:01:43.529
If three months ago somebody had said in the
next three months,

00:01:43.529 --> 00:01:47.509
one out of five Americans who is working is
going to lose their job.

00:01:47.509 --> 00:01:49.969
No one no one would have believed it.

00:01:49.969 --> 00:01:55.579
But if you force their hand to say, how do
you think the stock market would be doing?

00:01:55.579 --> 00:02:00.990
How do you think all these if they would say,
well, it would be absolute, complete devastation,

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:04.539
but nobody would have believed that that is
how bad things could possibly get in such

00:02:04.539 --> 00:02:05.600
a short period of time.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.739
But we are there now, previously before this
phase, before coronavirus.

00:02:09.739 --> 00:02:12.460
The largest one week job loss.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:19.850
New unemployment claims was somewhere in the
600000 range, maybe close to 700000.

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:26.879
We have done five to 10 times those numbers,
not in total during coronavirus each week

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:28.640
during the last seven weeks.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:30.469
And again, the total number is now.

00:02:30.469 --> 00:02:32.330
Thirty three and a half million jobs lost.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.150
Now, there are people who are

00:02:34.150 --> 00:02:38.020
seeing these numbers and they're in the camp
of open it.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.020
All right.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:42.799
Now, no matter what, to save the economy right
there, they're in that camp.

00:02:42.799 --> 00:02:47.239
And those folks see these numbers and they
say, here's the evidence, here's the evidence

00:02:47.239 --> 00:02:51.640
that we must reopen everything right now regardless
of the human cost.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:56.280
And by the way, the human cost is really big
anyway with things shut down, which is true.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:59.400
And that tells us just how contagious corona
viruses.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.200
That's a very short sighted perspective.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:02.590
It's not a good idea.

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:06.590
But I do believe that there are things that
we can glean from these numbers that should

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.670
inform what should have been done and also
how things should be handled going forward.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:16.280
First of all, even economies that appear to
be doing well, Trump has called the economy

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:18.390
up until seven weeks ago, the best economy
in the

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:20.310
history of any country ever.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:25.000
I don't call it that, but there's no doubt
that what we had up until seven weeks ago

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:31.209
was an economy that by the normal metrics
we use unemployment, inflation, consumer confidence,

00:03:31.209 --> 00:03:34.010
stock market, all of those measures, it was
doing well.

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:36.049
It was an economy that was doing well.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:40.469
But what we learned is even those economies
are extremely fragile.

00:03:40.469 --> 00:03:46.060
Even those economies can, on the drop of a
dime, make 180 degree turn.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:51.069
This fragility is inherent to our economy,
in part because of the marginal nature of

00:03:51.069 --> 00:03:55.379
a lot of the work in the economy, in part
because of the low savings rate, which is

00:03:55.379 --> 00:03:59.279
fueled by stagnant wages over a long period
of time.

00:03:59.279 --> 00:04:05.569
Dramatic inequality also gives you averages
that seem OK, even when a lot of individuals

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:08.540
are in a very precarious, precarious financial
state.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:09.540
The just in time

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.299
nature of many of our industries and supply
chains, all of these things should confirm

00:04:14.299 --> 00:04:18.970
to us that even when the economy looks pretty
good by the standard met metrics, it's a very

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:20.569
fragile economy that we have.

00:04:20.569 --> 00:04:21.569
That's part of the system.

00:04:21.569 --> 00:04:23.330
That's a feature of it, not a bug.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:31.090
Number two, what we also need to think about
is if you look at other countries right now,

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:37.819
if you look at Italy, France, Spain, a lot
of these hubs of cases, South Korea, of course,

00:04:37.819 --> 00:04:40.009
they are all on the dramatic decline.

00:04:40.009 --> 00:04:45.690
They are doing really, really well while we
are still having twenty five hundred deaths

00:04:45.690 --> 00:04:46.850
a day.

00:04:46.850 --> 00:04:50.900
And that leads me to have to talk about Donald
Trump's failure here.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:57.060
Now, I'm not going to do this completely irrational
thing where I could sit here and tell you

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:58.820
Trump, obviously.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.770
Have shut everything down on February

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:03.580
20th when we had 15 cases.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:08.770
Trump was very dumb and wrong to say on February
around, you know, late February we have 15

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:10.490
cases and it'll soon be zero.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.500
That was a dumb thing to say.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:16.090
No president is going to shut down a country
with 15 cases.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:19.490
I'm not going to sit here and tell you that
that's the logical thing that should have

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.560
been done at the time.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:25.050
But don't look at February 20th, look at March
5th, look at March 10th, OK?

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:28.830
On March 5th, we had 221 cases here in the
United States.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:34.199
On March 5th, China had 80000 cases proving
that this could go very, very big.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:36.139
But importantly.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:38.590
Italy had 4000 cases on March 5th.

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:42.759
So on March 5th, we knew 15 cases weren't
going to zero.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:44.490
We went to 21.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:47.729
We knew that a country could have close to
100000 cases.

00:05:47.729 --> 00:05:54.570
And we knew that another country could have
the disease seeded and already have 4000 cases.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.169
That's what we knew from Italy.

00:05:56.169 --> 00:06:02.509
So March 5th would not have been a crazy time
to say, hey, governors, all 50 of you, we

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:04.780
need to stay at home starting now.

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:08.730
If we had done that on March 5th, I'm not
saying February 20th.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:14.960
But if we had done it on March 5th, we would
now have done 60 days of it and we would have

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.440
had economic impacts similar to what we've
had.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:22.360
But we would be in very good shape right now
compared to where we are.

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:28.509
By mid March, when the sporadic stay at home
orders started in some states but not others,

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.750
the US had already been seeded with close
to 4000 cases of Corona virus.

00:06:33.750 --> 00:06:36.509
It was too late then to really stop the spread.

00:06:36.509 --> 00:06:41.750
It was especially not going to happen when
only some states started stay at home orders

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:42.980
in the middle of March.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:49.099
So don't fall for the trumpets talking point
that there were no cases in February and so

00:06:49.099 --> 00:06:52.420
it would have been crazy to shut it down then
they are right.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:57.509
I don't know of any president that with 15
cases would have demanded or asked 50 governors

00:06:57.509 --> 00:06:59.139
to shut things down.

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:03.250
But by early March, March 5, March 7, look
at the numbers for yourselves.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:07.439
We had the data that we needed to take it
seriously and Trump did not at that point

00:07:07.439 --> 00:07:08.439
in time.

00:07:08.439 --> 00:07:09.439
He'll brag.

00:07:09.439 --> 00:07:10.759
He shut down travel from China.

00:07:10.759 --> 00:07:13.900
But but clearly at that point, it had gone
outside of China

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.400
to a significant degree.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.400
And we now have 33 million jobs lost.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:23.599
But because of this rolling patchwork of shutdowns
in some states and not in others, we see some

00:07:23.599 --> 00:07:24.979
states improving, some worsening.

00:07:24.979 --> 00:07:27.690
We still have twenty five hundred deaths a
day.

00:07:27.690 --> 00:07:33.639
And FEMA and the CDC believe it is possible
that by June 1st, we will actually be up to

00:07:33.639 --> 00:07:35.919
three thousand deaths every single day.

00:07:35.919 --> 00:07:37.080
It's been mishandled.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.500
Let's be accurate in our criticism of how
it's been mishandled, but it has been dramatically

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.510
mishandled.

00:07:42.510 --> 00:07:46.129
And not only are we seeing a human toll.

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:48.979
We are also seeing an optional economic toll.

00:07:48.979 --> 00:07:53.229
We were going to have an economic impact,
but we could have grinned and buried it for

00:07:53.229 --> 00:07:55.999
60 days starting March 5th, and we would be
in much better shape right now.

